now it was of course the topic david  cameron warned his party some time back  to stop banging on abouts but the  clamors over europe have only got louder  and this week is of course crunch time  on thursday the prime minister will set  out his stall in brussels hoping for an  agreement on a package of reforms to  britain's relationship with the european  union well i'm joined now by one of the  most influential mps in parliament  graham brady as chair of the 1922  committee he is effectively the leader  of the conservative backbenchers now  very good morning to you mr brady right  well let's focus on brussels i mean is  it  i mean do you think it's really not  really worth bothering the prime  minister with this given you don't  really think that what he's got already  or the proto agreement with donald tusk  amounts to much i i think all the things  that david cameron's trying to get out  of the eu are good or positive things uh  but for many of us it just doesn't come  anywhere near to uh being sufficient i  wanted to see real  powers being brought back to british  control i wanted to see things taken out  of the hands of brussels and back in  british democratic control uh so  it doesn't come anywhere near that so  you kind of know he's not going to get  that because we as i say we saw one we  saw what he thrashed out with this is  down when he comes before his eu  partners who can say but as far as i'm  concerned what it doesn't constitute  even if he gets everything he's asking  for it isn't a fundamental change it  isn't the thing that brings real  democratic control back to the united  kingdom  restores our ability to decide who  governs us and to be able to kick people  out when we don't like them  but are there those that  perhaps didn't entirely support your  point of view thought okay  that's quite  quite a good agreement he he worked out  with the prime minister if they get  something like that then maybe i might  vote for him you're talking about my  colleagues i i certainly i never pretend  to speak for all of my background no  absolutely no no for equality especially  on this subject uh where there is a very  wide spectrum of views and of course  there'll be people who think that this  is a good deal but it's sufficient for  them  but i think many many people  in parliament and outside all over the  country are looking at this and thinking  is it really the fundamental change in  our relationship with the eu that we  wanted does it take us back  to what we thought we were joining 40  odd years ago does it take us back  to something which is really a trading  agreement and an ability to cooperate  with other sovereign states and clearly  it doesn't do that there is rumbling in  the background of all these negotiations  um noises coming from uh senior european  partners at the moment saying that there  will actually be be a price for the uk  should it  get agreement should it then vote to  remain within the european union that  you'll have to get more involved do you  have to become more of an active partner  well  the fundamental thing that we want and i  think i've always wanted with our  european partners is free and open trade  given that they sell more to us than we  sell to them i don't think there's any  danger of that free and open trade being  suspended they would be cutting off  their nose despite their face so i i  think it's a given that we would have  good open access to european markets  just as we would continue to allow good  open access to british markets for our  european uh partners but it's pretty odd  isn't it it's a pretty strange kind of  club  that we're a member of  if the other members as we try to change  our relationship are seeking to threaten  us it doesn't sound like such a good  arrangement as perhaps is sometimes  being suggested well the threats are not  just coming from our partners are there  i mean what do you think of the the  campaign because something clearly  underway at the moment you know there  are those uh on your side saying well  project fear has all ready started from  those that want us to remain within the  european union thinking about today's  story on the front of the sunday times  airfares will go up during the course of  last week we had five thousand migrants  will come here overnight yes from  california what do you make of this well  it's not so long ago that many of the  same people were telling us the british  economy would be finished if we didn't  join the euro so i think we should take  all of these things with a pinch of salt  but it is unfortunate i think that we're  not getting the kind of positive debate  that would be more welcome i think for  most of the british people and well  really  the question is is it a bigger leap in  the dark to stay in the european union  or are we taking a risk to to leave now  i think we know uh beyond doubt after  all of these decades of membership  that staying in the european union isn't  a  static position there's no status quo if  you're in this organization it carries  on integrating it carries on increasing  powers to itself it's done it throughout  its history uh it will continue to do  that but what those kind of stories do  tell us is is that it seems that  the stay in campaign and campaigns have  got their act together they're coming  across on an almost daily basis with a  different story whereas on the leave  side you've got various different  campaigns we know about the bickering  within the camps do you not think it  would be best to try and get one  organization to speak for you all well  of course we should have one  organization speaking for the leave  campaign i hope we'll get there soon i  hope that it'll happen uh through the  electoral commission uh getting on with  designation of a lead campaign we can  then all get on with the important thing  which is campaigning to restore british  democracy  but all of these campaigns all these  stories that have been coming out of the  campaign to remain  they may have been very good at  producing this copy but as soon as it  hits the front page as soon as it's on  the broadcast media it's all been  comprehensively rubbished so you know in  many ways i think the the more extreme  some of these stories become the more  the campaign to frighten people into  staying  gets into  into gear uh the more effectively it's  putting people off uh the european union  the more effectively it's making people  think there might be something a little  bit dodgy about this organization that  we're a member of and just um talk to me  about timings within your own party  within the government  after the brussels meeting should there  be a cabinet meeting as soon as possible  perhaps  next saturday to say to those that say  well prime minister you tried very hard  we appreciate all your efforts but you  didn't get enough please now let us  speak out to leave the european union i  think that's absolutely essential it's  in everybody's interest to do this as  quickly as possible and if it were to  appear that david cameron was seeking to  have the whole weekend to himself to put  one side of the argument  i think that would look bad for the  remain campaign people want an honest  fair  debate they want an honest fair campaign  so i think it's in the interest of both  sides to have that early cabinet meeting  and to make sure that people who want to  speak out uh people who want to exploit  that freedom of conscience that has  rightly been uh agreed uh can do so as  soon as possible that should take place  on saturday then uh well well let's look  at the timings if if you could take  place on friday evening that would be  even better okay and what about mr  cameron himself that poll uh out today  saying that you know he's been damaged  people losing confidence in mr cameron  on the eu negotiations i mean this is  the man of course you know won an  unexpected overall majority for your  party do you think it's chipping away at  him well i certainly think it's a  mistake to try to oversell this deal uh  it's very clear to everybody it's not a  fundamental change in our relationship  with the european union so mistake to  oversell it  but also looking at it in a more  positive way every time over the last  few years as prime minister david  cameron has done something on the  eurosceptic side every time he's stood  up for britain every time he has when he  vetoed the treaty when he managed to get  the eu budget capped uh all of those  occasions his popularity spiked uh so  let's hopefully draw an inference but i  mean you know he still technically could  uh could uh be against staying within  the european union very unlikely but if  he does campaign to remain and loses  that referendum as you would like to see  haven't you then ended his career i i  think uh the opposite uh really i think  it's essential uh that if and when we  vote to leave the european union i think  we should all seek to have david cameron  stay in office uh through what will be a  very important period of renegotiation  and you know i think that having that  degree of stability will be important  for the country and i don't need david  cameron to walk away uh overnight  because i think he's more responsible  than that i think he would know he'd  have a real commitment to the country  and a need to  make sure that we come through that that  period uh with maximum stability and  reassure everybody that we can do this  always great seeing you must end it  there mr brady thank you very much lee  graham brady there from the 1922  committee and you're watching monahan on  sky news coming up next

